<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Sign in with Apple. Required by App Store Review guideline 4.8 because the
	     app offers Google sign-in. Without this entitlement Apple's sheet refuses
	     to appear at runtime, and the matching capability must also be enabled on
	     the com.livil App ID in the developer portal - both halves are required. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Push notifications. Without this entitlement iOS refuses to issue an APNs token
	     and does so SILENTLY: registerForRemoteNotifications succeeds, no token arrives,
	     and device_tokens stays empty. That is the state this app shipped in — 48 Android
	     tokens and zero iOS. Firebase is configured and the JS registration path already
	     runs; the entitlement is the missing half.

	     `development` IS NOT "correct for TestFlight" — TestFlight builds are App Store
	     distribution builds and use the PRODUCTION APNs environment. Committing
	     `development` is still the conventional choice, but only because Xcode-managed
	     signing substitutes the value from the distribution provisioning profile at sign
	     time. That substitution is not yet guaranteed here: CODE_SIGN_STYLE is unset (so
	     Automatic by default) and Release still signs with "iPhone Developer". If signing
	     ever moves to manual or a CI pipeline with explicit profiles, the literal
	     `development` ships, the device registers against the APNs SANDBOX, FCM sends to
	     production, and every iOS push silently disappears — the same silence, from the
	     other direction, as the missing entitlement this fixes. Verify on a real archive:

	         codesign -d --entitlements :- <Release .app>   # must read `production`

	     The matching Push Notifications capability must ALSO be enabled on the com.livil
	     App ID in the developer portal, and an APNs auth key (.p8) uploaded to the Firebase
	     iOS app. Without that key getToken() fails and device_tokens stays empty with no
	     error — both halves are required, exactly as with Sign in with Apple above. -->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
</dict>
</plist>
